WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:03.560
In 'The Matrix Resurrections,' is Trinity
the One?

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.779
And why does Jada Pinkett-Smith's Niobe seem
to be so old?

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:09.734
Let's plug into the Matrix and dig into the
details."

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:14.080
Since the first trailer's release, speculation
has circulated as to what Jonathan Groff's

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.610
involvement in "The Matrix: Resurrections"
would be.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:20.230
His character was seen near the end of the
initial preview in a mic-dropping meta moment:

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:23.789
"Okay, we're going back to where it all started.

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.460
Back to the matrix!"

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.669
In that first trailer, the character certainly
gave off a huge "bad guy" vibe.

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:33.630
Now, following this all-new preview, there's
a lot to suggest that the former "Mindhunter"

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.410
could well be an upgraded version of the Agents
that Neo once faced before.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.149
Firstly, there's the near pitch-perfect impersonation
of Hugo Weaving's straight-faced baddie in

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:43.320
a necktie, Agent Smith.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.130
He also refers to Neo as "Mr. Anderson," the
name that Smith used to mock Neo's transition

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.269
to digital messiah.

00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:52.980
Could his part in this new chapter of "The
Matrix" be an advanced version of the Smith

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:53.980
program?

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.100
One that saw the real world and managed to
make his way back to the prison he couldn't

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.510
stand the smell of?

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.449
If so, it suits Groff perfectly.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.580
Neo may be the chosen one, but Trinity gets
a fair amount of attention in the final trailer

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.360
for "The Matrix Resurrections."

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.110
Compared to the previous two sequels, the
trailers suggest that this time around, Neo's

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.150
not the one in the driver's seat.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.910
There's one line in the second trailer that
seems to make this clear:

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:21.430
"The most important choice of Neo's life is
not his to make."

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:22.570
This is followed by Neo stating,

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:23.640
"She believed in me.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.810
It's my turn to believe in her."

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.560
Since Neo's first awakening as The One happened
because Trinity believed in him, it seems

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.090
safe to assume that Neo's referring to Trinity
here.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.560
The remainder of the trailer also centers
heavily on her, and we don't think that's

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.610
an accident.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:44.510
If this is a new version of the Matrix, is
it possible that Trinity's the new One?

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.110
Seen briefly in the last teaser with a copy
of "Alice in Wonderland," Priyanka Chopra's

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:55.210
as-yet unnamed character has a few more things
to say on the film's current events and, more

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.040
importantly, Neo's involvement in it all.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.720
But who is this person to have a say in our
hero's future?

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.410
Is she new to the scene, or could she be someone
from his past?

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.570
One common theory is that Chopra Jonas is
playing an older version of Sati, a character

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.209
who originally appeared in "The Matrix Revolutions."

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:14.410
Sati was a program that looked like a child,
built without purpose by her parents who were

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.480
smuggled into The Matrix by the Oracle.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.569
For her part, the Oracle believed Sati would
play a massive part in the future for both

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:21.989
the machines and humanity.

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:24.189
Could this be the role the Oracle saw for
her?

00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:25.189
"That's it.

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:28.280
You've got to use your hands."

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.280
"Why?"

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.400
"Cookies need love like everything does.”

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.030
Debuting in "The Matrix Reloaded," Niobe,
portrayed by Jada Pinkett-Smith, played a

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.319
critical role in the events of the second
two "Matrix" films.

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:42.860
Although her character took on a supporting
role in the movies, she was featured prominently

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.920
as a main character in the video game tie-in
"Enter the Matrix," which was written by the

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.569
Wachowskis and is considered to be canonical.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:53.989
The 2003 video game fleshed out the character,
along with her co-pilot Ghost, and showed

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:57.890
how critical their actions were in making
sure Neo, Morpheus, and Trinity's plans come

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.080
to fruition.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.860
Niobe was able to survive the onslaught of
the machines at the end of "Matrix Revolutions,"

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.739
and her character embraced the newfound peace
between machines and humanity.

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:10.430
In the new "Matrix Resurrection" trailer,
we see a woman who seems to be an older Niobe

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.999
— a much older Niobe, which raises questions
about when this sequel is supposed to take

00:03:14.999 --> 00:03:16.069
place.

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:20.930
Whether she's the Niobe we remember or a computer
program or a new character entirely played

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.129
by the same actress, we have no idea.

00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:26.870
Either way, no shot in the trailer makes it
clearer just how much time has passed since

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.569
the story of "The Matrix" started.

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:31.639
"I don't know if you're ready to see what
I want to show you."

00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:35.849
In the original "Matrix" films, scenes taking
place within the Matrix tend to be tinted

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:40.659
green, whereas scenes set in the real world
are largely dark blue or gray in color.

00:03:40.659 --> 00:03:44.609
This was especially prominent in "Reloaded"
and "Revolutions," when both Neo and the audience

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:48.209
were fully aware of the artificial nature
of the Matrix's reality.

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:51.909
In the trailers for "The Matrix Resurrections,"
though, the shots that seem to take place

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:55.480
in a version of the Matrix are saturated with
a distinct blue color.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.739
Rather than recalling the cold and gray real
world of the original three "Matrix" films,

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:03.650
this color seems more representative of the
blue pill famously presented to Neo in the

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.129
first "Matrix" film.

00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:10.800
"You take the blue pill, the story ends, you
wake up in your bed and you believe whatever

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.719
you want to believe."

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:16.810
Since the first trailer for "The Matrix Resurrections"
showed Neo taking what seemed to be a daily

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:21.660
dose of blue pills, it seems like this movie's
false reality might be an artificial world

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.100
that, for Neo at least, is in need of constant
reinforcement.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.570
One exception to the predominance of blue
shades in what seems to be the Matrix occurs

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.440
during a haunting shot in the second trailer
that seems to show Neo walking off the edge

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:33.500
of a building.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.100
In this shot, Neo looks to be choosing to
defy the reality around him with a leap of

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.280
faith, as desperate onlookers scream and reach
for him.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.840
As Neo walks toward the sunlight on the left
side of the frame, the clothing on the people

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.340
and the colors of the chairs behind them seem
to be an equal mix of red and blue, perhaps

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.290
indicating Neo's gradual turn away from a
blue-pilled way of thinking.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.420
This red-blue mix also seems to be at play
in the trailer's shots of the red pods that

00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:00.320
hold the bodies of people plugged into the
Matrix -- the towers of human batteries that

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.880
Morpheus originally described in the first
movie.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.050
In the trailer's shots of these people pods,
the colors blue and red co-mingle, seeming

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:11.010
to demonstrate a clash between the false reality
of the machines, and the real world beyond

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.010
the Matrix.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:15.950
While the fourth Matrix movie seems to be
a continuation of the previous story, it appears

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:20.280
that it will once again involve the deconstruction
of a false reality in which Neo and Trinity

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.630
have become somehow entangled.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.410
"Maybe this isn't the story we think it is."

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:28.000
The reality that Neo seems to be trapped in
during "The Matrix Resurrections" could very

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.360
well represent a state of blissful ignorance,
with the machines or some other force keeping

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.240
Neo sedated in a fake world for reasons we
don't know yet.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.750
That idea of ignorance would also explain
why Neo doesn't seem to have any memory of

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.100
the events we know occurred with him before.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.960
From what we've seen of "Resurrections," making
Neo remember what happened in the past seems

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.180
to be key to the story's future.

